WEBVTT

1
00:00:15.352 --> 00:00:19.973
It was close to mid-morning, and Sherlock Holmes and I were in the middle of interviewing Mr.

2
00:00:20.013 --> 00:00:22.494
Wilson about his fantastic part-time job.

3
00:00:23.454 --> 00:00:28.796
He had gotten this job not because of any skill or talent, but simply because he had red

4
00:00:28.856 --> 00:00:34.333
hair. He had worked there for eight weeks, and received a handsome salary of four pounds per week.

5
00:00:35.034 --> 00:00:35.654
But Mr.

6
00:00:35.694 --> 00:00:41.095
Wilson had gone to work that morning only to find his office closed and his job gone.

7
00:00:42.056 --> 00:00:43.036
He had come to Mr.

8
00:00:43.096 --> 00:00:49.038
Holmes looking for answers, and my friend, upon hearing the story, perceived that there was more to this

9
00:00:49.118 --> 00:00:51.518
part-time job than anyone had initially thought.

10
00:00:52.539 --> 00:00:58.920
Mr. Wilson had just finished explaining how his assistant had brought the notice to his attention; Mr.

11
00:00:58.980 --> 00:01:01.841
Spaulding had even offered to accompany him to the interview.

12
00:01:01.861 --> 00:01:03.842
"I see.

13
00:01:04.362 --> 00:01:10.545
First, your assistant convinced you that the advertisement was valid, and then, he proposed going with you to

14
00:01:10.585 --> 00:01:12.466
the interview," said Holmes.

15
00:01:12.486 --> 00:01:15.627
"Now, can you describe the interview for me?"

16
00:01:15.647 --> 00:01:16.987
"Oh, Mr.

17
00:01:17.047 --> 00:01:20.109
Holmes, I never want to see such a spectacle again.

18
00:01:21.089 --> 00:01:23.390
Men with red hair came from every direction.

19
00:01:24.130 --> 00:01:28.272
I think every red-haired man in the city of London answered the advertisement.

20
00:01:29.272 --> 00:01:33.095
If I had been alone, I certainly would have returned home, but Mr.

21
00:01:33.156 --> 00:01:35.277
Spaulding was quite sure I would succeed."

22
00:01:36.418 --> 00:01:38.980
"Indeed," said Holmes encouragingly.

23
00:01:39.000 --> 00:01:40.602
"Please continue."

24
00:01:40.622 --> 00:01:44.245
"Well, we waited and eventually we made it inside.

25
00:01:44.665 --> 00:01:48.268
The office was quite bare: a couple of wooden chairs and a card table.

26
00:01:48.889 --> 00:01:51.511
In the office was a man with hair redder than mine.

27
00:01:51.531 --> 00:01:52.772
‘This is Mr.

28
00:01:52.812 --> 00:01:54.494
Wilson,' said my assistant.

29
00:01:55.074 --> 00:01:57.096
And the man seemed very pleased to see me.

30
00:01:57.728 --> 00:01:59.349
He introduced himself as Mr.

31
00:01:59.369 --> 00:02:02.150
Duncan Ross and explained that the late Mr.

32
00:02:02.210 --> 00:02:03.951
Hopkins had been his benefactor.

33
00:02:04.711 --> 00:02:06.512
Then he grabbed my hair and pulled it."

34
00:02:07.632 --> 00:02:08.693
"And what happened next?"

35
00:02:09.473 --> 00:02:10.373
I said impatiently.

36
00:02:10.393 --> 00:02:13.635
"Oh, I cried out," stated Mr.

37
00:02:13.655 --> 00:02:15.095
Wilson. "Mr.

38
00:02:15.135 --> 00:02:21.118
Ross apologized, saying, ‘Sorry, but we have twice been tricked by wigs and once by paint.'

39
00:02:21.938 --> 00:02:28.117
He then shouted to those still waiting, ‘The position has been filled,' and the crowd let out a

40
00:02:28.177 --> 00:02:28.857
great groan.

41
00:02:29.658 --> 00:02:34.080
Mr. Ross stated that he would be my manager and proceeded to tell me about the job.

42
00:02:34.981 --> 00:02:38.823
I would have to be in the office from ten to two every Monday through Saturday.

43
00:02:39.343 --> 00:02:44.366
Furthermore he said that I could not leave the office for any reason during that time, or I

44
00:02:44.406 --> 00:02:49.970
would lose the job, and while I was in the office, I should copy the Encyclopedia Britannica."

45
00:02:51.010 --> 00:02:53.872
Mr. Wilson paused, then continued his story.

46
00:02:54.708 --> 00:02:58.071
"When I told him I already had a job, he didn't seem concerned.

47
00:02:58.811 --> 00:03:02.094
He simply asked, ‘Is there no one who could run it for you?

48
00:03:02.294 --> 00:03:03.074
A son, perhaps?

49
00:03:03.715 --> 00:03:08.618
Before I could answer, my assistant exclaimed, ‘I could look after the business for you, Mr.

50
00:03:08.658 --> 00:03:09.979
Wilson.' Mr.

51
00:03:10.039 --> 00:03:11.841
Ross said, ‘Excellent!

52
00:03:11.861 --> 00:03:12.581
Then it's settled.

53
00:03:13.422 --> 00:03:16.404
I expect you here on Monday by ten,' and he left.

54
00:03:17.285 --> 00:03:21.148
Now, a pawnshop's business is mostly conducted in the evenings, Mr.

55
00:03:21.168 --> 00:03:26.285
Holmes especially on Thursdays and Fridays when people find themselves in need of cash.

56
00:03:27.185 --> 00:03:30.766
So a part-time job in the mornings and early afternoons suited me well.

57
00:03:31.747 --> 00:03:36.848
Besides I knew my assistant was a competent man, and so for the last eight weeks, I have

58
00:03:36.868 --> 00:03:41.610
been working my way through the encyclopedia, starting at the first volume, A.

59
00:03:42.490 --> 00:03:44.830
But today it has all come to an end."

60
00:03:45.971 --> 00:03:51.973
Mr. Wilson reached into his pocket and pulled out a small printed card that said: THE RED-HEADED LEAGUE

61
00:03:52.273 --> 00:04:00.219
IS DISSOLVED October 9, 1890 Holmes and I looked at the curt announcement and the bewildered face of

62
00:04:00.279 --> 00:04:01.780
the disappointed man who held it.

63
00:04:02.981 --> 00:04:05.402
I then looked at Holmes, and Holmes looked at me.

64
00:04:06.303 --> 00:04:11.467
As we stared at each other, the comical nature of the entire affair made us burst into a

65
00:04:11.527 --> 00:04:12.327
roar of laughter.

66
00:04:12.347 --> 00:04:15.529
"I don't find this at all amusing," said our client.

67
00:04:15.549 --> 00:04:18.952
"If you are only going to laugh at me, I shall go elsewhere!"

68
00:04:19.964 --> 00:04:23.347
"No, no," said Holmes, gently guiding Mr.

69
00:04:23.367 --> 00:04:24.487
Wilson away from the door.

70
00:04:24.507 --> 00:04:28.210
"I don't want to lose this case, so please excuse us.

71
00:04:28.650 --> 00:04:31.392
Can you tell us what you did after reading the announcement?"

72
00:04:32.433 --> 00:04:37.356
"First I asked around at some of the adjacent offices, but they were ignorant of Mr.

73
00:04:37.396 --> 00:04:39.958
Ross and the Red-Headed League," answered Mr.

74
00:04:39.998 --> 00:04:45.622
Wilson. "I then went to the landlord, and he too had never heard of them, so I asked

75
00:04:45.682 --> 00:04:47.744
about the gentleman from room number four.

76
00:04:48.468 --> 00:04:54.611
The landlord told me that the red-headed man's name was William Morris and that his lease had expired,

77
00:04:55.171 --> 00:04:56.511
so he moved out yesterday.

78
00:04:57.572 --> 00:05:03.594
I asked where I could find him, and the landlord gave me the following address: 17 King Edward

79
00:05:03.614 --> 00:05:08.796
Street. I was determined to find out what was going on, so I made for that address at

80
00:05:08.996 --> 00:05:14.238
once. But when I got there, I found that it was an umbrella factory, and no one had

81
00:05:14.278 --> 00:05:15.339
heard of either Mr.

82
00:05:15.399 --> 00:05:17.220
William Morris or Mr.

83
00:05:17.260 --> 00:05:17.960
Duncan Ross."

84
00:05:19.004 --> 00:05:20.045
"What did you do next?

85
00:05:20.505 --> 00:05:21.865
Did you come straight here?"

86
00:05:22.405 --> 00:05:22.906
asked Holmes.

87
00:05:22.926 --> 00:05:26.147
"No, I stopped at home," answered Mr.

88
00:05:26.167 --> 00:05:29.708
Wilson. "My assistant suggested that I wait for the mail.

89
00:05:30.149 --> 00:05:34.230
He thought a formal letter must have been sent in the mail, but I am not a patient

90
00:05:34.250 --> 00:05:36.531
man, so I decided to come to you."

91
00:05:36.551 --> 00:05:44.814
"Yes, that is wise," said Holmes, "for I believe something quite serious may be about to happen.

92
00:05:45.695 --> 00:05:47.135
Can you tell me more about Mr.

93
00:05:48.460 --> 00:05:50.662
Spaulding? For example, how did you hire him?"

94
00:05:51.582 --> 00:05:54.324
"He answered my advertisement," said Mr.

95
00:05:54.364 --> 00:05:57.066
Wilson. "Were there other applicants?"

96
00:05:57.086 --> 00:06:03.511
"Many others, but he indicated that he would be willing to work for half wages," replied Mr.

97
00:06:03.551 --> 00:06:05.872
Wilson. "What does he look like?"

98
00:06:06.493 --> 00:06:06.973
asked Holmes.

99
00:06:06.993 --> 00:06:09.195
"He is small and dynamic.

100
00:06:09.635 --> 00:06:11.917
And there is a small scar on his forehead."

101
00:06:12.537 --> 00:06:13.858
Holmes sat up excitedly.

102
00:06:13.878 --> 00:06:16.080
"Are his ears pierced?"

103
00:06:17.320 --> 00:06:18.401
"Yes. Do you know Mr.

104
00:06:18.421 --> 00:06:19.861
Spaulding?" asked Mr.

105
00:06:19.881 --> 00:06:20.762
Wilson, surprised.

106
00:06:20.782 --> 00:06:26.584
"No," said Holmes, "but a man of that description is known to me.

107
00:06:27.304 --> 00:06:28.445
I think we're finished, Mr.

108
00:06:28.465 --> 00:06:31.386
Wilson. I hope to have your answer by Monday."

109
00:06:32.446 --> 00:06:33.587
Holmes accompanied Mr.

110
00:06:33.607 --> 00:06:35.868
Wilson to the door, and we said our good-byes.

111
00:06:37.028 --> 00:06:41.590
"Well, Watson," said Holmes, turning to me, "can you make sense of it?"

112
00:06:42.951 --> 00:06:45.412
"I can make nothing of it," I answered frankly.

113
00:06:46.280 --> 00:06:47.321
"What do you plan to do?"

114
00:06:47.341 --> 00:06:51.404
"Smoke," said Holmes, pulling his pipe from his pocket.

115
00:06:52.085 --> 00:06:55.507
He sat down in his chair, and contemplated the problem at hand.

116
00:06:56.328 --> 00:07:02.512
From experience, I knew not to interrupt him during these periods of concentration, so I read a magazine

117
00:07:02.552 --> 00:07:03.013
and waited.

118
00:07:03.993 --> 00:07:09.057
The magazine was dull and I was feeling a little drowsy, so I was quite startled when Holmes

119
00:07:09.137 --> 00:07:10.658
suddenly sprang from his chair.
